      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Threaded Binary Tree\n====================",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Threaded Binary Tree\n====================",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "h7VhNHYAojGzd8LYXXOZ-",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 65,
      "y": 215,
      "width": 823,
      "height": 375,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a1",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 271526806,
      "version": 832,
      "versionNonce": 1546764438,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1793263218936,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1. Normally in Binary Tree not every pointer of each node is used .\n\n2. Leaf node and nodes which have only one child stores null in their both and\nsingle pointer .\n\n3. But if we replace this null with some meaningful addreses then we AVOID using\nstack during tree traversal .\n\n4. Such a tree which replaces nulls with some meaningful addresses is called a\nthreaded binary tree ,\n\n5. They are of three types of each traversal order , so overall we have 9 types of\nTBT .\n\n6. However most usefull one are TBT's based on INORDER .",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1. Normally in Binary Tree not every pointer of each node is used .\n\n2. Leaf node and nodes which have only one child stores null in their both and single pointer .\n\n3. But if we replace this null with some meaningful addreses then we AVOID using stack during tree traversal .\n\n4. Such a tree which replaces nulls with some meaningful addresses is called a threaded binary tree ,\n\n5. They are of three types of each traversal order , so overall we have 9 types of TBT .\n\n6. However most usefull one are TBT's based on INORDER .",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "KyldzjeAK_fSdGMntNr8V",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 65,
